riot games is paying out 100 million  dollars in a settlement to women who  experience sexual harassment and  discrimination while working for the  company now that sounds like a lot of  money and it is but the story is a  little bit more complicated than riot  just finally doing something decent when  it comes to this whole situation  let's talk about it  so on december 27th the washington post  shannon liao reported that riot games  the california department of fare  employment and housing the california  division of labor standards enforcement  and quote certain claimants had reached  a 100 million dollar settlement about a  2018 lawsuit regarding gender  discrimination that took place at the  company in plain english riot is paying  out 100 million dollars to women who  experience sexual harassment and  discrimination while working at riot  games this is obviously a big deal but  there's a lot of context that we have to  lay out before we talk about what this  means and what happens next starting at  the very beginning in august 2018 kotaku  published a report by cecilia donastasia  alleging that there was a culture of  sexist attitude and gender  discrimination at riot games the article  included quotes from then current and  former ride employees about how women  were often paid less than a minute riot  given less raises than men at riot and  were given fewer promotions than men at  riot a few months later in november 2018  then current ride employees just negron  and melanie mccracken filed a lawsuit  alleging that there was gender  discrimination and sexual harassment  occurring at riot games and were seeking  damages this was followed a few months  later by riot attempting to force women  into private arbitration which was  followed by employees walking out in  protest in may and the dfvh announcing  they were looking into riot in december  2019 wright agreed to pay 10 million  dollars to settle the lawsuit and  committed to improving company culture  but then the dfeh and dlsc stepped in to  say that they believe the women involved  in the class-action lawsuit actually  deserved 400 million dollars and blocked  ryan from paying out the 10 million they  did this according to court filings  because the dlsc stepped in to say that  they believed the lawyers representing  the women at ryan did not do their due  diligence and could have quote let right  off the hook for breaking more labor  laws which basically brings us up to  today after months of pretty much radio  silence riot is now paying out 100  million dollars to these women now that  is 10 times what they originally agreed  to pay but it's also a quarter of what  the california state agencies originally  said they should be paying 80 million  dollars is going directly to the women  suing riot while the other 20 million is  going to pay their legal fees in a  statement riot's executive team stated  that the reason they're paying out so  much more now is because there are more  parties involved there are more lawyers  which increases the legal fees but more  importantly the class suing riot has  grown a lot since the original lawsuit  was filed basically in a class action  lawsuit a group of people who were  wronged by another party are considered  a class and what started as a relatively  small class in this particular lawsuit  grew a lot since ryde agreed to pay the  10 million back in 2019. see now it  covers all people who identify as women  who worked full time or in temporary  contract positions at riot since  november 2014. basically riot is saying  one of the reasons they're paying out so  much more money is because there are a  lot more people suing them now now to be  clear a settlement like this is not an  admission of guilt we'll get to how riot  feels about what went down in a bit but  for now what you have to understand is  that this isn't right admitting that  they broke labor laws in fact in their  statement riot's executive team says  this given the ambitious goals we have  for camp 2 and the tens of millions of  dollars we'd spend each year on lawyers  to help resolve these cases money we'd  rather pay to the women in this class  and to invest in riot's future it became  clear during these past several months  that the best outcome for everyone would  be to come to a final resolution  effectively what riot is saying here is  that if they were gonna fight this in  court the legal fees were gonna balloon  like crazy so it'd be cheaper and better  honestly to just pay out money to the  women who are allegedly discriminated  against or sexually harassed while  working at riot this is a pretty common  reason for settlements by the way  somebody realizes that a protracted  court battle is going to be a lot  messier and a lot more expensive than  just paying out the money right now and  making the lawsuit go away and to be  clear just because i sound super cynical  about all this doesn't mean i think this  is terrible or something i think it's  genuinely great that riot has to pay out  10 times what they originally agreed and  that it seems like women are going to be  receiving repertory funds for the harm  they experience while working at riot  but riot is still referring to the  lawsuit as being around alleged gender  harassment and discrimination occurring  at the company while also saying this we  also know that at the end of the day  this issue isn't just about legal  arguments it's about human beings and  their individual experiences while we're  proud of how far we've come since 2018  it's important that we also take  responsibility for our past that began  with an apology to rioters because  despite our best intentions we haven't  always lived up to our values we  followed with significant upgrades to  our people in dni practices and now  today with a proposal for meaningful  repair remember a settlement isn't an  admission of guilt a court never found  that gender discrimination took place at  riot games and a court never found that  sexual harassment occurred at riot games  in the eyes of the law riot isn't guilty  of any of this which is why they're  using some really careful language in  this statement to avoid admitting that  they potentially broke the law however a  lot of people perceive settlements like  this as admissions of guilt in the court  of public opinion we're all free to  perceive whatever we want to but to be  clear riot isn't admitting anything all  they're saying is that some bad stuff  has happened at riot games offices over  the years and they feel really bad about  that a hundred million dollars bad  specifically anyway i brought all that  up to sort of highlight the big thing  for me here see riot is shelling out all  this money we're not gonna have a big  protracted court battle the women who  were hurt are getting money i think all  of that is good basically but what's  more nebulous is what happens next the  law firms representing the women suing  riot are quoted in the statement  basically saying it's a great day for  women in the games industry and that  hopefully this marks a trend moving  forward of women demanding fair  treatment of pay notably we've been  hearing stories about similar things  happening at other major game studios  like activision blizzard and ubisoft all  of this has happened and in some cases  might still be happening at some studios  but what are the next steps riot talks  in the statement about investing in the  future but what does that look like  riot's ceo nicola laurent is quoted in a  statement and he's currently embroiled  in a sexual misconduct lawsuit his  former executive assistant is suing him  for allegedly behaving improperly  towards her and while riot has cleared  him internally after a third party  investigation found that he didn't do  anything the lawsuit alleges sharon  o'donnell his former assistant has  doubled down on her claims scott gelb  who according to a separate kotaku  report by anastasio allegedly walked  around the office doing things like  grabbing people's balls humping  employees as a joke and farting on  people was suspended without pay back in  2018  but then in january 2021 he was promoted  to president of games at riot these are  two names that have been alleged of  fostering the kind of attitude at riot  that led to this lawsuit in the first  place i'm not going to say that progress  is impossible with folks like these two  at the helm because one these  allegations have not been proven in a  court of law so that's all they are  right now allegations but two i have to  believe that people can change and grow  otherwise we're all [ __ ] but you can't  deny that the optics here are a little  bit screwy riot talks a big game in the  statement about how far they've come  since 2018 and how they're gonna invest  in their future to make it a better  place to work meanwhile it takes about  five seconds of googling to see all the  horrible things their senior staff has  been accused of ultimately settlements  like this are often backed with ndas so  this story probably is over for now the  people suing riot will likely be asked  to sign papers guaranteeing their  silence about this issue in the future  that means that if riot is actually  doing the work and things are changing  internally we're probably not going to  hear anything no news is good news in  this case but if things don't change or  if they somehow get worse than what's  been reported then those ndas might  start getting broken or newer employees  will become the whistleblowers and at  that point it'll be pretty clear that  there's a real problem here  you
 

